Q. Can we insert only selected columns?
Yes. We can specify only the required columns in the INSERT statement. Any omitted columns will either take their default value or NULL, depending on the table definition.

Q. Can INSERT create duplicate records?
Yes, unless the table has constraints such as a PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE constraint to prevent duplicates.

Q. What happens if you insert a duplicate primary key?
The database returns an error because the PRIMARY KEY must be unique. The new record will not be inserted.

Q. Can INSERT insert NULL values?
Yes, provided the column allows NULL values and does not have a NOT NULL constraint.


UPDATE STATEMENT:

The UPDATE statement is used to modify existing records in a database table. It allows us to change one or more column values for specific rows based on a condition.
In my current role, I use UPDATE statements to perform approved data corrections, user updates,
and configuration changes after validating the request and ensuring the correct records are identified.

UPDATE Employee
SET Salary = 75000
WHERE EmpID = 101;

| Keyword | Meaning                        |
| ------- | ------------------------------ |
| UPDATE  | Table you want to modify       |
| SET     | Column(s) to change            |
| WHERE   | Which row(s) should be updated |

If the WHERE clause is omitted, the UPDATE statement modifies every row in the table. 
This can cause serious production issues. That's why I always verify the target records using a SELECT query before executing an UPDATE.


UPDATE Employee
SET Salary = 70000,
    Department = 'Finance'
WHERE EmpID = 101;

Can UPDATE modify multiple rows?
Yes. If multiple rows satisfy the WHERE condition, all matching rows will be updated.

UPDATE Employee
SET Salary = Salary + 2000
WHERE Department = 'HR';


DELETE statement:

The DELETE statement is used to remove existing records from a table based on a specified condition. It deletes only the data, not the table structure. 
In production, I use DELETE only after proper approval and verification because deleting data is an irreversible action unless a backup or transaction rollback is available.

DELETE FROM Employee
WHERE EmpID = 101;

If the WHERE clause is omitted, all records in the table are deleted. Therefore, I always verify the records using a SELECT query before executing a DELETE statement in production.

I have worked with production databases, but permanent deletion is generally controlled through change management and approvals.
Before deleting any record, I verify the data, confirm the request, execute the DELETE only after approval, and validate the result.

Q.A customer requests that an employee record be deleted?
First, I would verify the employee record using a SELECT query. Then I would confirm that the deletion request has the necessary business approval. After ensuring the correct record is identified,
I would execute the DELETE statement using an appropriate WHERE condition. Finally, I would verify that the record has been removed and update the customer.

Q. A manager asks you to delete all inactive users.
I would first identify the users by running a SELECT query with the same WHERE condition to verify the affected records. 
After approval, I would execute the DELETE statement and then validate that only the intended users were removed.


| Feature               | DELETE               | TRUNCATE       | DROP                    |
| --------------------- | -------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------- |
| Removes               | Selected rows        | All rows       | Entire table            |
| WHERE clause          | ✅ Yes                | ❌ No           | ❌ No                    |
| Table structure       | Remains              | Remains        | Deleted                 |
| Can delete one record | ✅ Yes                | ❌ No           | ❌ No                    |
| Use case              | Remove specific data | Clear all data | Remove table completely |

TRUNCATE TABLE Employee; - Deletes every row.
DROP TABLE Employee; --The table no longer exists.

DELETE removes rows based on a condition and supports the WHERE clause. TRUNCATE removes all rows from a table and does not support WHERE.  ****
The table structure remains in both cases, but TRUNCATE is generally faster because it removes all data at once.


"In production, I treat DELETE as a high-risk operation. I first validate the records using a SELECT query, confirm the business approval, 
execute the DELETE with a precise WHERE condition, and then verify that only the intended records were removed."

